<p>A London jury convicted Chi Leung “Peter” Wai, a UK Border Force officer, and Chung Biu “Bill” Yuen, a senior manager at the Hong Kong Economic and Trade Office in London and retired Hong Kong police officer, of assisting a foreign intelligence service under Britain’s National Security Act at the Old Bailey on May 7. Prosecutors said the two dual Chinese-British nationals carried out surveillance and gathered intelligence on Hong Kong pro-democracy dissidents and protesters in Britain in what was described in court as a “shadow policing” operation. Wai was also convicted of a separate misconduct in a public office count. The jury failed to reach a verdict on a foreign interference count linked to a May 1, 2024, forced-entry allegation at the Pontefract home of Monica Kwong, and prosecutors said they would not seek a retrial on that charge. After the verdicts, Britain said it would summon China’s ambassador, while Chinese and Hong Kong officials rejected the allegations.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/jlD5uscBZ6lrnv_W5l8gxNi8i2ELmif0AnKYIja_yBw6tUt0nLARn545pEAswwPw3NvgbYDoW_flFxQpt_mImbDSjtCKgsM-VBIVismPOX5qum6g7L65IWZHW1COUJN2WmM58eE32-M5OejQBd8xGZ1VRV8XPI6DXQVQHJwZr5PJvRBb87zsW37boEJmxlbaAdQZokznZcTh7nuVrDdXJzsgMDevmVkGO2UKCSIQ3vSGpJ8SPVKvqBJldA' alt='Bill Yuen, left, and Peter Wai were convicted of assisting a foreign intelligence service under the National Security Act.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Jury decision: The 12-person jury deliberated for 23 hours and 38 minutes; The Collective reported that the assisting-a-foreign-intelligence-service verdicts were 10-2 and Wai’s misconduct verdict was 12-0.</li><li>Third defendant: Matthew Trickett, a third suspect charged in the case, was found dead in a park in May 2024 after he was released on bail, and local police said an inquest was expected after the criminal proceedings ended.</li><li>Police evidence: London’s Metropolitan Police said investigators reviewed more than 20 terabytes of data, including thousands of messages, while examining the alleged surveillance network.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>UK government: Security Minister Dan Jarvis said Britain would summon the Chinese ambassador after the convictions for spying on behalf of Hong Kong and, ultimately, China. (<a href='https://www.dailymaverick.co.za/article/2026-05-08-uk-to-summon-chinese-ambassador-after-spying-convictions/'>Daily Maverick</a>)</li><li>Chinese embassy in the UK: Beijing’s embassy dismissed the case as a “political move” and rejected the allegation that the men acted for Chinese authorities. (<a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/hong-kong/law-and-crime/article/3352809/uk-court-convicts-2-men-linked-hong-kong-trade-office-spying'>South China Morning Post</a>)</li><li>Hong Kong government: The Hong Kong government said the allegations were “absolutely” unrelated to the administration and maintained that its London trade office had carried out its duties under UK law. (<a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/hong-kong/law-and-crime/article/3352809/uk-court-convicts-2-men-linked-hong-kong-trade-office-spying'>South China Morning Post</a>)</li><li>Metropolitan Police: Investigating officer Helen Flanagan said the conduct uncovered by police was “sinister” and “frightening,” and that the investigation found the men had targeted democracy campaigners in Britain for Hong Kong authorities. (<a href='https://thecollectivehk.com/%e8%8b%b1%e5%80%ab%e8%ab%9c%e6%a1%88%ef%bd%9c%e8%a2%81%e6%9d%be%e5%bd%aa%e3%80%81%e8%a1%9e%e5%bf%97%e6%a8%91%e5%8d%94%e5%8a%a9%e5%a4%96%e5%9c%8b%e6%83%85%e5%a0%b1%e6%a9%9f%e9%97%9c%e7%bd%aa%e6%88%90/'>The Collective</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/hong-kong/law-and-crime/article/3352809/uk-court-convicts-2-men-linked-hong-kong-trade-office-spying'>UK court convicts 2 men linked to Hong Kong trade office of spying</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2026/may/07/two-men-guilty-spying-for-c